course: reflection
lesson: 26
title: The mind at night: replays, worst-case thinking and nightmares
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: At night, when the light goes off and there is nothing else to look at, the mind often plays its own reruns: every night Jamie's brain showed the same clip of him saying "I love you, miss" to his teacher instead of "thank you, miss", and he went hot under the duvet: "Why does my brain do this? It's like a rubbish TV channel I can't switch off."
explain: In the day he was fine; at night the clip came again and again; after a week it started to feel boring rather than awful; after three weeks it barely came at all, because each replay is the brain trying to "file" the moment, and each one makes it a little less sharp.
explain: Trying hard NOT to think about it made it louder; what helped was Dad saying he once called his teacher "Mum" at secondary school, and saying to himself, "Oh, that clip again," then thinking about his favourite football match instead.
explain: The night mind has other tricks: it jumps from one cross voice to "I'll be alone forever", it doubts the locked door, it turns sad days into nightmares, it makes a coat look like a monster; in daylight, with facts, almost all of it looks much smaller.
explain: Most of these tricks are normal and fade on their own; but if a rule like "touch the switch five times or Mum gets hurt" keeps growing and taking time, it is a sign to tell a grown-up, because there is real help.
fact: The mind often replays embarrassing moments at night because it is quiet and there is little else to think about; a "cringe attack" is slang for suddenly remembering something embarrassing.
fact: Replays usually get less sharp over days and weeks, especially if you let them pass instead of fighting them.
fact: Trying hard not to think about something usually makes the thought come back more; noticing it and turning to something else works better.
fact: Catastrophising means jumping from a small thing to the worst possible ending; "spiralling" is when thoughts get worse and worse, and "making a mountain out of a molehill" means making a small problem huge.
fact: Writing a worry down at night and checking in the morning what really happened shows how often night worries were too big.
fact: Doubts like "did I lock the door?" feel very real at night; checking once and saying out loud "I checked, it's locked" helps more than checking again and again.
fact: Nightmares are the brain sorting through big feelings, often after something sad or scary; they are not warnings and they do not predict the future.
fact: Memories are not videos; they are rebuilt each time we remember, so two people can honestly remember the same moment differently.
fact: The spotlight effect is the feeling that everyone notices you far more than they really do; most people are busy thinking about themselves.
fact: When we feel bad, the mind notices bad things more; listing three good things each evening trains it to notice good things too.
fact: Big world worries like climate change can feel huge at night; doing small useful things, like an eco club, eases the helpless feeling.
fact: OCD is a condition where worries make people repeat actions or follow strict rules; if such rules grow and take up time, telling a grown-up is right because doctors can help.
fact: In the dark the brain fills unclear shapes with familiar or scary things, like faces; seeing faces in objects is called pareidolia, and a night light helps.
exercise: Jamie's brain replays his "I love you, miss" moment every night. Why does it happen at night? | It's quiet and there's nothing else for the mind to look at | His brain is broken somehow and needs to be fixed by a doctor soon | The teacher is thinking about it every night too | Embarrassing things only ever happen in the evening
exercise: What happened to Jamie's replay over three weeks? | It got boring, then barely came | It got louder every single night | It turned into a real, scary nightmare | It stayed exactly the same every night
exercise: What made the replay LOUDER for Jamie? | Trying hard not to think about it | Saying "oh, that clip again" | Thinking about his favourite football match instead | Hearing Dad's own story
exercise: A girl says "Did you hear me?" crossly, and Ruby ends up sure she'll be alone forever. What is this called? | Catastrophising | Daydreaming about the future | Remembering | Sleepwalking
exercise: What helped Ruby with night worries? | Writing the worry down and checking it in the morning | Going over the worry all night until it was solved | Asking the girl at midnight if she was still cross | Never speaking to that girl again just in case
exercise: Leo checked the back door three times and still doubted it. What helped? | Checking once and saying "I checked, it's locked" | Getting up again and again until morning | Sleeping on the floor right next to the back door | Locking every door ten times each
exercise: After grandad's funeral, Mia dreams every night that Mum dies too. What are nightmares? | The brain sorting through big feelings, not warnings | Messages from the future that tell us what will happen next | Proof that something bad is about to happen | Signs that Mia is a bad person
exercise: What helped Mia's nightmares? | Talking about them and drawing a happy ending | Staying awake all night so she couldn't dream | Watching scary films to toughen up | Never telling anyone what she dreamt
exercise: Noah and his friend remember their fight completely differently. Both are sure. How can that be? | Memories are rebuilt each time and feelings bend them | One of them must be lying on purpose | Memories are exact videos of what happened, so one must be wrong | Only grown-ups' memories can be trusted
exercise: Ellie is sure everyone is staring at her red eyes. What is this called? | The spotlight effect | The red-eye effect of crying | The mirror rule of faces | The curse of the crowd
exercise: After a bad day Kofi thinks, "Everything always goes wrong for me. I'm cursed." What helped? | A "three good things" list before bed | Listing everything that went wrong | Buying a lucky charm to break the curse | Staying in bed all weekend
exercise: Lily lies awake about climate change after a TV programme. What helped? | Joining an eco club and doing one small thing each week | Reading the scary news every night before bed to be ready | Deciding nothing can ever be done | Never watching any TV again
exercise: Ben must touch the light switch five times or "something bad will happen to Mum". What should he do? | Tell a grown-up; that can be helped | Touch it ten times to be extra safe | Hide it, because it is silly | Keep doing it, since Mum is fine
exercise: Max knows monsters aren't real but sees a face in his coat in the dark. Why? | The brain fills dark shapes with familiar or scary things | Monsters really do hide in wardrobes | His coat has a real face printed on the back of it | He is too young to know what is real
exercise: What helped Max? | A night light and moving the coat | Being told off for being so scared | Sleeping with the wardrobe door open | Watching monster films right before bed
test: Jamie's mate goes, "bro that's a cringe attack, everyone gets 'em." ("Cringe attack" means suddenly remembering something embarrassing.) Is he right? | Yes; replaying embarrassing moments is very common | No; only Jamie's brain does this, nobody else's | No; it means Jamie is ill | Yes, but it never fades
test: Ruby's sister says at breakfast, "Mate, you're spiralling, she literally said hi to you." ("Spiralling" means thoughts getting worse and worse.) What does this show? | Night worries often look much smaller in daylight | Ruby was right all along, and the girl really does hate her now | Ruby should worry more to be safe | Saying hi means the girl is plotting
test: Leo checks the door five times. What would probably happen if he kept doing it every night? | It could become a habit that feeds the worry | The doubt would disappear for good after enough checks | The house would become totally safe | He would sleep much better each time
test: Mia's mate says, "my nan says dreams come true, so watch out." What's the truth? | Nightmares are the brain sorting feelings, not predictions | Her nan is right; bad dreams always come true | Only the dreams you have on a Friday night come true | Dreams come true if you dream them twice
test: Noah's teacher shows the video of the playground fight and it's "more mixed". What does this teach? | Two people can honestly remember one moment differently | Videos always lie about what happened | Noah was lying all along | Memories are always perfect, so the video must be wrong
test: Ellie's mate from Dublin says, "Ah, sure nobody even noticed, you're grand." ("Grand" means fine.) What's the effect called that Ellie felt? | The spotlight effect | The Irish luck effect | The red-eye trick of tears | The law of the mirror
test: Kofi's dad from Lagos says, "Ah, you are not cursed, my son, tell me one good thing." What is the dad doing? | Training Kofi's mind to notice good things too | Telling him curses are real, but only weak ones that wear off | Punishing him for being negative | Ignoring how bad Kofi's day was
test: Lily goes, "the planet's literally ending, it's so over." ("It's so over" means there's no hope.) What helps more than lying awake? | Small useful things, like an eco club | Reading more scary news every night | Deciding nothing anyone does matters | Staying awake every night until she solves it herself
test: Ben's mate says, "touching the switch is just a quirk, everyone does it." When should Ben tell a grown-up? | When it keeps growing | Never; rules like that always fix themselves | Only if the light switch breaks | Only if his mum finds out first
test: In a cartoon, the kid's coat turns into a real monster at night. What's actually happening in real rooms? | The brain fills dark shapes with scary things | Coats really can come alive in the dark | Monsters only appear to children, so grown-ups never see them | Night lights attract monsters
test: Jamie decides to fight the replay: "DON'T think about it, DON'T think about it." What will probably happen? | The thought will come back even more | The thought will vanish forever at once | He will forget his teacher's name | He will fall asleep straight away
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises nightmares experienced after a bereavement? | They reflect the brain's processing of strong feelings and do not foretell events | They constitute reliable predictions of future misfortune | They are experienced only by persons of poor character or weak nerve | They indicate that a further bereavement is imminent
exam: Identify the principal consequence of a deliberate effort to suppress an unwanted thought. | The thought tends to recur with greater frequency | The thought is permanently erased from memory | The thinker is rendered unable to sleep thereafter | The thought is transferred to another person
exam: It is given that two pupils recall a single incident in contradictory terms. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Memory is reconstructed upon recall and may honestly differ between persons | One pupil must necessarily be lying with intent to deceive | Memory constitutes an exact recording of past events | Only the elder pupil's recollection can be regarded as accurate
exam: In respect of a self-imposed rule requiring repeated actions to avert harm to another, determine which statement is correct. | Where such a rule grows and consumes time, disclosure to an adult is advisable | Such a rule is a reliable means of protecting others from harm | Such rules ought to be concealed from all adults lest the pupil be thought foolish | Such rules invariably disappear upon reaching adulthood
